2	 * AI — navigation and cover.
3	 *
4	 * NAVIGATION is a dense walkability grid sampled straight out of the physics
5	 * BVH at boot: one downward ray per cell finds the floor, one upward ray checks
6	 * standing clearance, and the floor normal gives the slope. That is a navmesh's
7	 * worth of information for a fraction of the code, and it stays correct for a
8	 * level the `world` system generated procedurally without any authoring pass.
9	 *
10	 *   • A* over the 8-connected grid with a heap, slope and step penalties
11	 *   • string pulling against a line-of-walk test, so paths hug corners instead
12	 *     of zig-zagging cell to cell
13	 *   • per-agent local avoidance so a squad flows around itself
14	 *
15	 * COVER is derived from the same grid. Every walkable cell next to a blocker
16	 * becomes a cover point with a direction and a height class (full / crouch),
17	 * plus a peek offset that has line of sight past the edge. At runtime cover is
18	 * scored against the live threat direction, the agent's distance, and what the
19	 * rest of the squad has already claimed.
20	 */
